WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Lecturer: Today we talk about use cases and why we write them before code.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.000
Lecturer: A use case describes one goal of one actor, step by step.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.000
Lecturer: The main flow is the normal path, where everything works.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:25.000
Lecturer: Alternative flows describe errors and other choices, for example a wrong password.

5
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.000
Student: Should the use case say which buttons are on the screen?

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.000
Lecturer: Usually not. It says what the actor does and what the system does.

7
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Lecturer: Preconditions say what must be true before the use case starts.

8
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.000
Lecturer: Postconditions say what is true after it ends, for example which data is stored.

9
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.000
Lecturer: From each flow we can write at least one test case. That gives traceability.

10
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:06.000
Lecturer: If a requirement has no test, we do not know whether it works.

11
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:13.000
Lecturer: For homework, write one use case for borrowing a book from a library.

12
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.000
Lecturer: Add two alternative flows. That is all for today.
